    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently analyzing a collection of ceramic shards from the late Helladic IIIA strata. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between the firing profiles of these fragments—some show a very high degree of vitrification while others seem much more porous despite appearing to come from the same context. What specific indicators should I look for in the fabric and surface treatment to determine if these were fired using different kiln techniques?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To distinguish between different firing profiles, examine the density and sound of the pottery; dense rings that ring loudly indicate high temperature vitrification, whereas dull, heavy sounds suggest lower temperatures and higher porosity. Look for surface lustres such as a glassy shine versus a matte finish, as heat treatment profoundly affects how minerals within the clay react and melt. Additionally, check for thermal cracks or differential sintering between layers, which often reveals inconsistent heat exposure despite the fragments having appeared in the same context. Carefully document these physical properties to correlate specific kiln environments with your stratigraphic data.",
      "source": "target",
